    rdfs:label "Bloom filters cost the node work and, as Gervais and others showed, give the node a view of the client's addresses with weak privacy, since a node that gets the filter can often work out which addresses belong to the wallet ..."@en .

pg:Q5_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A transaction can be shown to be in a block with a Merkle path: the transactions of a block are hashed in pairs up to a single root that sits in the header, and the proof for one transaction is the hash of each sibling on the ..."@en .

pg:Q5_O3 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The book says that the mempool and the orphan pool are a node's local view and may differ from node to node, whereas the UTXO set is the shared outcome of consensus (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q6_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The book says that the mempool and the orphan pool are a node's local view and may differ from node to node, whereas the UTXO set is the shared outcome of consensus (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q6_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A transaction can be shown to be in a block with a Merkle path: the transactions of a block are hashed in pairs up to a single root that sits in the header, and the proof for one transaction is the hash of each sibling on the ..."@en .

pg:Q6_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "Bloom filters cost the node work and, as Gervais and others showed, give the node a view of the client's addresses with weak privacy, since a node that gets the filter can often work out which addresses belong to the wallet ..."@en .

pg:Q7_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A transaction can be shown to be in a block with a Merkle path: the transactions of a block are hashed in pairs up to a single root that sits in the header, and the proof for one transaction is the hash of each sibling on the ..."@en .

pg:Q7_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The book says that the mempool and the orphan pool are a node's local view and may differ from node to node, whereas the UTXO set is the shared outcome of consensus (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q7_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "Bloom filters cost the node work and, as Gervais and others showed, give the node a view of the client's addresses with weak privacy, since a node that gets the filter can often work out which addresses belong to the wallet ..."@en .

pg:Q8_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The book says that the mempool and the orphan pool are a node's local view and may differ from node to node, whereas the UTXO set is the shared outcome of consensus (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q8_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "Bloom filters cost the node work and, as Gervais and others showed, give the node a view of the client's addresses with weak privacy, since a node that gets the filter can often work out which addresses belong to the wallet ..."@en .

pg:Q8_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The seventh section is about the unconfirmed transactions of a node: how they are announced, how a peer filters them, why every mempool is local, and what happens to a child that arrives before its parent, which changed with ..."@en .

pg:Q9_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "Bloom filters cost the node work and, as Gervais and others showed, give the node a view of the client's addresses with weak privacy, since a node that gets the filter can often work out which addresses belong to the wallet ..."@en .

pg:Q9_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The book says that the mempool and the orphan pool are a node's local view and may differ from node to node, whereas the UTXO set is the shared outcome of consensus (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q9_O3 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The seventh section is about the unconfirmed transactions of a node: how they are announced, how a peer filters them, why every mempool is local, and what happens to a child that arrives before its parent, which changed with ..."@en .

pg:SectionTree a ipo:TreeNavigation,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Section tree"@en ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10/document> ;
    skos:definition "The section tree lists the sections of the chapter by their three levels, and choosing one opens that single section."@en .

pg:Sidebar a ipo:Sidebar,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Chapter explorer"@en ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10/document> ;
    skos:definition "The explorer at the left shows the chapter's sections as a tree and keeps pace with the tabs of the main area."@en .

pg:SourceDocument a ipo:SourceDocument,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Chapter 10 Stage 3 document"@en ;
    ipo:hasLearningObjective pg:CO1,
        pg:CO2,
        pg:CO3,
        pg:CO4,
        pg:CO5,
        pg:CO6 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10/document> ;
    skos:definition "The Stage 3 document of chapter 10 is the text this page renders, section for section."@en .

pg:TopMenu a ipo:PrimaryNavigation,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Main menu"@en ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10/document> ;
    skos:definition "The main menu is permanently visible at the top and lets the reader reach every top-level area without scrolling or opening anything first."@en .

pg:Q10_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The original transport of the Bitcoin peer-to-peer protocol, now called v1, sends every message as plain bytes: anybody on the path, a router, a provider or a coffee shop's Wi-Fi, can read which transactions and blocks a node ..."@en .

pg:Q11_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "BIP 324 defines the v2 transport, in which the two sides first make a shared secret with a key exchange whose public keys are encoded with ElligatorSwift so that they look like random bytes, and then send every message ..."@en .

pg:Q12_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A node does not send a whole transaction to every peer: it announces it with an inv message that holds only the identifier, and the peer asks with getdata if it does not have it already; since BIP 339 the identifier is the ..."@en .

pg:Q1_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The Bitcoin network is a peer-to-peer network: every node is a peer, there is no central server, no privileged coordinator, and a node that wants to join needs only the address of one other node (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:Q2_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A reachable node is one that accepts incoming connections, and the crawler Bitnodes counts them by trying to connect to every address it learns about, so its number is a lower bound of the nodes that exist, since a node behind ..."@en .

pg:Q3_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "Every message of the Bitcoin peer-to-peer protocol on the old transport is a block of bytes with a header of 24 bytes: 4 bytes of magic that name the network, 12 bytes with the command padded with zeros, 4 bytes with the ..."@en .

pg:Q4_O3 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The first message that two peers exchange is version, in which each side states its protocol version, its services, the time, the addresses it sees, a random nonce to detect a connection to itself, its user agent, the height ..."@en .

pg:Q5_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "Blocks are found by chance at an average of one in ten minutes, and while a new block travels through the network, other miners are still working on the old tip; if one of them finds a block in that time, two blocks compete ..."@en .

pg:Q6_O3 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "In the original protocol a node announces a new block to its peers with an inv message that holds only the hash of the block; the peer must answer with getheaders or getdata, and only then does the header arrive, which costs a ..."@en .

pg:Q7_O3 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A new node first downloads the headers of the whole chain and only then the blocks, which is called headers-first synchronization; the node asks with getheaders and the peer answers with at most 2,000 headers in one headers ..."@en .

pg:Q8_O3 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A lightweight client does not download the blocks; it downloads only the block headers, 80 bytes per block, and asks other nodes for proof that a payment is in a block, an idea that Satoshi Nakamoto described as simplified ..."@en .

pg:Q9_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A transaction can be shown to be in a block with a Merkle path: the transactions of a block are hashed in pairs up to a single root that sits in the header, and the proof for one transaction is the hash of each sibling on the ..."@en .

pg:S_AddrGossipFormat a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Addr gossip format"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#AddrGossipFormat" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_AddrGossipFormat> ;
    skos:definition "In answer to getaddr the node returned 23 addresses, the same number in the old addr message and in the addrv2 message, which is 23 percent of the 103 addresses of its address manager as MAX_PCT_ADDR_TO_SEND says."@en .

pg:S_Addrv2Networks a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Addrv2 networks (BIP 155)"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Addrv2Networks" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_Addrv2Networks> ;
    skos:definition "Once a peer sent sendaddrv2 the node answered with addrv2 messages whose entries belong to networks 1 and 2 (IPv4 and IPv6) with address lengths 4 and 16 bytes, and BIP 155 adds TORV3, I2P and CJDNS entries that the old 16-byte addr message could not hold."@en .

pg:S_AnonymityNetworks a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Anonymity networks"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#AnonymityNetworks" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_AnonymityNetworks> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks how a node can hide where it is: the five networks, Tor with its automatic onion service, and the later I2P and CJDNS (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_ArchivalNodeServices a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Archival node services"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ArchivalNodeServices" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_ArchivalNodeServices> ;
    skos:definition "A full archival node of the test network advertises the services value 3081 (hexadecimal c09), which decodes to NETWORK, WITNESS, NETWORK_LIMITED and P2P_V2; the book's description of archival full nodes matches the first of these."@en .

pg:S_BasicFilterParameters a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Basic filter parameters (BIP 158)"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BasicFilterParameters" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_BasicFilterParameters> ;
    skos:definition "BIP 158 sets P = 19 and M = 784931 for the basic filter, the constants of the independent implementation, which reproduced all 10 test vectors of the BIP (filters and filter headers) and the SipHash reference value a129ca6149be45e5."@en .

pg:S_BlockFilters a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Block filters"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BlockFilters" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_BlockFilters> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks how block filters turned the question around: the node gives a compact filter of each block and the client decides what to download, with the Golomb-coded set, the parameters of BIP 158, the size of the filters and the history of the idea (Osuntokun and Akselrod, 2017)."@en .

pg:S_BlockFindingRaceProbability a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Block finding race probability"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BlockFindingRaceProbability" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_BlockFindingRaceProbability> ;
    skos:definition "While a block is travelling a rival block can be found: with blocks as a Poisson process of one per 600 seconds the chance is 0.33 percent for a 2-second delay, 1.65 percent for 10 seconds and 6.45 percent for 40 seconds."@en .

pg:S_BlockRelay a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Block relay"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BlockRelay" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_BlockRelay> ;
    skos:definition "The third section is about how a new block gets through the network quickly, because the network must carry a block within seconds if rival blocks are to remain rare: announcement with headers, compact blocks and the private networks of the miners (Corallo, 2016)."@en .

pg:S_BloomDisabledByDefault a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Bloom disabled by default"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BloomDisabledByDefault" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_BloomDisabledByDefault> ;
    skos:definition "A node started with default options does not set the BLOOM bit and disconnects a peer that sends filterload; the option -peerbloomfilters defaults to 0 since v0.19.0, and only 4,308 of 25,361 nodes of the census advertise the bit."@en .

pg:S_BloomFilterWireFormat a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Bloom filter wire format (BIP 37)"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BloomFilterWireFormat" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_BloomFilterWireFormat> ;
    skos:definition "The filterload message of three elements and false-positive rate 0.01 is 03614e9b050000000000000001 in the unit test of Bitcoin Core and in the independent implementation, and a filter may be at most 36,000 bytes with at most 50 hash functions."@en .

pg:S_BloomFilters a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Bloom filters"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BloomFilters" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_BloomFilters> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks how a light client asked a full node for its transactions with a Bloom filter, how the filter works, what the messages look like and why the service was switched off by default (Hearn and Corallo, 2012)."@en .

pg:S_CoinbaseIsPrefilled a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Coinbase is prefilled"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CoinbaseIsPrefilled" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_CoinbaseIsPrefilled> ;
    skos:definition "The compact block of the run listed exactly one prefilled transaction, at index 0, and it was the coinbase transaction, because no peer can have it in its mempool."@en .

pg:S_CompactBlockDepthLimit a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Compact block depth limit"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CompactBlockDepthLimit" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_CompactBlockDepthLimit> ;
    skos:definition "A compact block is answered only for a block at most 5 blocks deep (MAX_CMPCTBLOCK_DEPTH), so a request for a block 20 deep was answered with the full block."@en .

pg:S_CompactBlocks a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Compact blocks"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CompactBlocks" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_CompactBlocks> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks how a block can be sent without repeating what the receiver has already: short ids of 6 bytes, a prefilled coinbase, a request for what is missing, two modes of sending and the depth limit, and it corrects the year of the book (Corallo, 2016)."@en .

pg:S_CompactBlocksYear a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The year of compact blocks"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CompactBlocksYear" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_CompactBlocksYear> ;
    skos:definition "The book dates the compact blocks to a new Bitcoin Core release of 2015, while the BIP 152 text was assigned on 2016-04-27 and Bitcoin Core documents the BIP as implemented since v0.13.0, so the year in the book is an error."@en .

pg:S_ConnectionTypes a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Connection types"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ConnectionTypes" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_ConnectionTypes> ;
    skos:definition "The peer listing of the node shows the connection types outbound-full-relay and addr-fetch, which relay transactions and addresses (relaytxes true), and block-relay-only, which relays neither and was on the v1 transport in the run, while the source sets 8 full-relay outbound slots, 2 block-relay-only slots and 125 connections in all."@en .

pg:S_Connections a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Connections"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Connections" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_Connections> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks which kinds of connection a node keeps and why they differ: full relay, block-relay-only, addr-fetch and the others, with the limits that Bitcoin Core sets (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_Discovery a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Discovery"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Discovery" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_Discovery> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks how a new node finds its first peers and how it learns more: DNS seeds, the exchange of addresses with getaddr and the addrv2 format that holds the long addresses of Tor, I2P and CJDNS (Van der Laan, 2019)."@en .

pg:S_DnsSeedList a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "DNS seed list"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#DnsSeedList" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_DnsSeedList> ;
    skos:definition "Bitcoin Core 31.1 names 8 DNS seeds in chainparams.cpp and resolving them gave 182 distinct IPv4 addresses, from 20 to 29 per seed, for the default port 8333; the sandbox could not open a raw TCP connection to any of them, so no mainnet peer was contacted."@en .

pg:S_DownloadWindow a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Download window"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#DownloadWindow" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_DownloadWindow> ;
    skos:definition "A new node downloads blocks inside a window of 1,024 blocks (BLOCK_DOWNLOAD_WINDOW) with at most 16 in transit from one peer; a fresh node synchronised a chain of 2,105 blocks from one peer in about 2.5 seconds, and its getheaders request was 69 bytes."@en .

pg:S_EclipseAttack a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Eclipse attack"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#EclipseAttack" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_EclipseAttack> ;
    skos:definition "The eclipse attack of Heilman and colleagues surrounds a victim with the attacker's addresses; the paper's title and the anchor-connection defence both stand in the saved copies."@en .

pg:S_EncryptedTransport a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Encrypted transport"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#EncryptedTransport" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_EncryptedTransport> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks what an observer can see of a connection: everything on the old transport, almost nothing on the v2 transport of BIP 324, which is on by default since release 27.0 and which still does not say who the peer is (Mehta et al., 2019)."@en .

pg:S_EncryptedV2 a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Encrypted v2 transport (BIP 324)"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#EncryptedV2" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_EncryptedV2> ;
    skos:definition "On the v2 transport of BIP 324 neither the magic nor the word version appears in the stream, the first 4,096 bytes have 7.953 bits of entropy per byte (of at most 8), and both sides hold the same 64-character session identifier."@en .

pg:S_FeefilterHonoured a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Feefilter honoured"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FeefilterHonoured" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_FeefilterHonoured> ;
    skos:definition "A peer that sent feefilter for 5,000 satoshis per kilovirtual byte (5 per virtual byte) was announced only the transaction of 20.0 satoshis per virtual byte and not the one of 2.0, and the node's own feefilter to its peer was 100 satoshis per kilovirtual byte, 0.1 per virtual byte."@en .

pg:S_FilterBitsPerElement a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Filter bits per element"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FilterBitsPerElement" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_FilterBitsPerElement> ;
    skos:definition "The filter of the run took 275 x 8 / 104 = 21.15 bits per element, where the book speaks of a filter that fits down to just 64 bits per element."@en .

pg:S_FilterEqualsNodeFilter a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Filter equals the node's filter"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FilterEqualsNodeFilter" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_FilterEqualsNodeFilter> ;
    skos:definition "The independent implementation built the filter of a block of 9 transactions, 104 elements in 275 bytes, and got the same 275 bytes and the same filter header as the node's getblockfilter."@en .

pg:S_FilterFalsePositiveRate a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Filter false positive rate"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FilterFalsePositiveRate" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_FilterFalsePositiveRate> ;
    skos:definition "A filter with M = 784931 is expected to match a random script once in 784,931 trials; in 400,000 trials of the run it matched once, where 0.51 matches are expected, and every script of the block matched."@en .

pg:S_FilterIdeaPost2016 a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Filter idea post of 2016"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FilterIdeaPost2016" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_FilterIdeaPost2016> ;
    skos:definition "The idea of a filter that the client downloads and tests locally came from an anonymous post to the bitcoin-dev list on 2016-05-09 and was written up as BIP 157 and 158 by Olaoluwa Osuntokun and Alex Akselrod, as the book says."@en .

pg:S_FilterSkipsOpReturn a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The filter skips OP_RETURN"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FilterSkipsOpReturn" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_FilterSkipsOpReturn> ;
    skos:definition "The block of the run had 2 OP_RETURN outputs, none of them is in the filter, and its 144 scripts gave 104 elements after the OP_RETURN scripts and the duplicates were left out."@en .

pg:S_FiltersOverP2P a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Block filters over the peer-to-peer network"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FiltersOverP2P" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_FiltersOverP2P> ;
    skos:definition "A node started with -peerblockfilters=1 advertises COMPACT_FILTERS, answers getcfilters with 3 filters equal to the block's filter, and a node without the option disconnects the asking peer; 7,905 nodes of the census advertise the bit."@en .

pg:S_FiveNetworks a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Five networks"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FiveNetworks" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_FiveNetworks> ;
    skos:definition "The node lists five networks, ipv4 and ipv6 reachable and onion, i2p and cjdns not reachable without a proxy; a node started with a proxy reported onion as reachable at 127.0.0.1:9."@en .

pg:S_FlatNetwork a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Flat network"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FlatNetwork" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_FlatNetwork> ;
    skos:definition "The first subject asks what kind of network Bitcoin is: a flat peer-to-peer network without a centre, how many nodes there are today, which program they run and which services they say they offer (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_ForwardErrorCorrectionDemo a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Forward error correction demonstration"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ForwardErrorCorrectionDemo" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_ForwardErrorCorrectionDemo> ;
    skos:definition "A message of 24 bytes cut into four parts of 6 bytes and one parity part of 6 bytes (the XOR of the four) was restored completely after one part was lost, which is the simplest form of the forward error correction that FIBRE applies to UDP packets."@en .

pg:S_GolombRiceRoundTrip a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Golomb-Rice round trip"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#GolombRiceRoundTrip" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_GolombRiceRoundTrip> ;
    skos:definition "Golomb-Rice coding of the five numbers with parameter 7 takes 44 bits (6 bytes), against 50 bits of a fixed length of 10 bits, and decodes back to the same numbers."@en .

pg:S_GolombWorkedExample a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Golomb worked example"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#GolombWorkedExample" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_GolombWorkedExample> ;
    skos:definition "Sorting the five numbers of the book's example gives 379 476 653 849 900, whose differences 379 97 177 196 51 need at most 8 bits where the originals need 10, and the book's table of the differences of two dice (6, 10, 8, 6, 4, 2 out of 36) is right."@en .

pg:S_Handshake a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Handshake"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Handshake" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_Handshake> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks how two peers who have never met start to talk: the frame of every message, the version message, the protocol number, the order of the first messages and the flag that asks for silence about transactions (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_HandshakeMessageOrder a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Handshake message order"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HandshakeMessageOrder" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_HandshakeMessageOrder> ;
    skos:definition "A node that accepts a connection says version first, then the feature messages wtxidrelay and sendaddrv2, then verack, and after verack it sends sendcmpct, ping, getheaders, inv and feefilter."@en .

pg:S_HeaderChainLinks a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Header chain links"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HeaderChainLinks" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_HeaderChainLinks> ;
    skos:definition "Walking the 2,101 downloaded headers, each one pointed at the hash of its predecessor and the last one hashed to the tip of the node; each header is 80 bytes."@en .

pg:S_HeaderChainSize a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Header chain size"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HeaderChainSize" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_HeaderChainSize> ;
    skos:definition "At the height 971,499 of the chain the 80-byte headers total 77.7 megabytes against about 775,272 megabytes for the whole chain, 9,975 times less, so the book's remark that the headers are about 10,000 times smaller is correct to three digits."@en .

pg:S_HeadersBatchOf2000 a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Headers in batches of 2,000"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HeadersBatchOf2000" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_HeadersBatchOf2000> ;
    skos:definition "A node with a chain of 2,101 blocks served the headers in a first message of 2,000 headers and a second of 101, each header taking 81 bytes (80 and a zero transaction count), and the constant MAX_HEADERS_RESULTS is 2000."@en .

pg:S_HeadersFirst a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Headers first"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HeadersFirst" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_HeadersFirst> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks how a new node catches up with the chain: headers first, in batches of 2,000, then the blocks inside a window, with a protection against floods of headers (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_HeadersPresync a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Headers presync"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HeadersPresync" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_HeadersPresync> ;
    skos:definition "Bitcoin Core 31.1 puts a peer that sends headers in a PRESYNC phase until the chain is shown to have enough work, and it reports presynced_headers in getpeerinfo, a field that the book's listing already shows."@en .

pg:S_HighAndLowBandwidthModes a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "High and low bandwidth modes"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#HighAndLowBandwidthModes" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_HighAndLowBandwidthModes> ;
    skos:definition "The node counted the script as a high-bandwidth peer (bip152_hb_to true, bip152_hb_from false in its peer listing) and sent it the compact block unasked; in low-bandwidth mode it announced nothing by cmpctblock, the script's request for the compact block was answered, and that answer equalled the unasked one; the source limits the high-bandwidth peers to 3."@en .

pg:S_I2pAndCjdns a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "I2P and CJDNS"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#I2pAndCjdns" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_I2pAndCjdns> ;
    skos:definition "Support for I2P came in release 22.0 and for CJDNS in 23.0, neither of which the book names, and the census holds 4,208 I2P nodes and one CJDNS node."@en .

pg:S_LightClients a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Light clients"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#LightClients" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_LightClients> ;
    skos:definition "The fifth section is about nodes that do not keep the chain: simplified payment verification with headers and Merkle paths, the Bloom filters of BIP 37 that have been switched off, and the compact block filters of BIP 157 and 158 that replaced them (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_MempoolAndOrphans a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Mempool and orphans"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MempoolAndOrphans" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_MempoolAndOrphans> ;
    skos:definition "The seventh section is about the unconfirmed transactions of a node: how they are announced, how a peer filters them, why every mempool is local, and what happens to a child that arrives before its parent, which changed with the orphan handling of releases 29 to 31 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_MempoolDefaults a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Mempool defaults"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MempoolDefaults" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_MempoolDefaults> ;
    skos:definition "The mempool of Bitcoin Core 31.1 holds at most 300,000,000 bytes, expires transactions after 336 hours, takes replacements of any transaction (fullrbf true) and limits a cluster to 64 transactions."@en .

pg:S_MempoolIsLocalUtxoIsShared a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The mempool is local, the UTXO set is shared"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MempoolIsLocalUtxoIsShared" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_MempoolIsLocalUtxoIsShared> ;
    skos:definition "Two connected nodes held 6 and 0 transactions in their mempools before a block (the second was started blocks-only and told the first not to send it transactions), and after the block both had the same UTXO set (107 outputs, the same hash), so the mempool is local and the UTXO set is shared."@en .

pg:S_MempoolRelay a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Mempool relay"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MempoolRelay" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_MempoolRelay> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks how unconfirmed transactions travel: the announcement by wtxid, the minimum fee rate a peer asks for, the difference between a local mempool and the shared UTXO set and the defaults of the mempool (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_MerklePathProof a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Merkle path proof"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MerklePathProof" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_MerklePathProof> ;
    skos:definition "A block of 5 transactions was proven for one of them by 3 hashes whose root equals the Merkle root of the header; the proof took 215 bytes against 4,448 for the block, and the node's verifytxoutproof returned the transaction."@en .

pg:S_MerkleblockForOneMatch a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Merkleblock for one match"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MerkleblockForOneMatch" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_MerkleblockForOneMatch> ;
    skos:definition "With a filter loaded the node sent one merkleblock of 215 bytes for the block of 5 transactions (4,448 bytes) and then the one matching transaction, and the partial Merkle tree led to the root of the header."@en .

pg:S_MessageFraming a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Message framing"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MessageFraming" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_MessageFraming> ;
    skos:definition "Every message starts with a 24-byte header of magic, command, length and checksum, so the empty verack message is the 24 bytes fabfb5da76657261636b000000000000000000005df6e0e2 with the magic of the test chain, whereas the main chain uses f9beb4d9, the four bytes that chainparams.cpp sets."@en .

pg:S_MissingTransactionsRequest a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Missing transactions request"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MissingTransactionsRequest" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_MissingTransactionsRequest> ;
    skos:definition "When the receiver lacked two transactions it asked for them with getblocktxn and the node answered with blocktxn holding exactly those two transactions, so the block could be completed."@en .

pg:S_NetworkAndNodes a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Network and nodes"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#NetworkAndNodes" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_NetworkAndNodes> ;
    skos:definition "The first section of the chapter explains that Bitcoin runs on a peer-to-peer network of nodes that are equal, and that nodes differ in the services they offer, such as keeping the whole chain or only the latest part (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_NetworkAttacks a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Network attacks"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#NetworkAttacks" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_NetworkAttacks> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks how the network itself can be attacked: the eclipse attack on the choice of peers and the routing attack on the internet below the network (Heilman et al., 2015)."@en .

pg:S_NodeKinds a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Node kinds"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#NodeKinds" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_NodeKinds> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks how nodes differ in what they keep: the archival node that has all of the history and the pruned node that has only the latest, and how they say it with service bits (Schnelli, 2017)."@en .

pg:S_NodeRequestsMissingParent a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Node requests missing parent"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#NodeRequestsMissingParent" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_NodeRequestsMissingParent> ;
    skos:definition "After the orphan the node asked the announcing peer for the parent with getdata type 0x40000001 (MSG_WITNESS_TX), and since release 29.0 it asks all the peers that announced the orphan."@en .

pg:S_OrphanPool a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Orphan pool"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#OrphanPool" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_OrphanPool> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks what a node does with a transaction whose parent it does not know: it keeps it, asks for the parent, releases it when the parent comes and limits the memory it may use, with limits that changed in releases 29, 30 and 31 (Bitcoin Core developers, 2025)."@en .

pg:S_OrphanWaitsForParent a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Orphan waits for parent"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#OrphanWaitsForParent" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_OrphanWaitsForParent> ;
    skos:definition "A child whose parent was not known was asked for by wtxid (inv type 5), did not enter the mempool and was kept in the orphanage, where one entry of 191 bytes was listed."@en .

pg:S_OrphanageLimits a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Orphanage limits"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#OrphanageLimits" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_OrphanageLimits> ;
    skos:definition "Release 30.0 limits the orphanage by entries plus a tenth of their inputs (at most 3,000) and by a total of 404,000 weight units times the number of peers, and release 31.0 removed the option -maxorphantx that the old limit of 100 orphans used."@en .

pg:S_ParentReleasesOrphan a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Parent releases orphan"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ParentReleasesOrphan" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_ParentReleasesOrphan> ;
    skos:definition "When the parent arrived both parent and child were in the mempool and the orphanage was empty again."@en .

pg:S_PeersAndDiscovery a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Peers and discovery"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PeersAndDiscovery" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_PeersAndDiscovery> ;
    skos:definition "The second section teaches how a node joins: it finds first peers by DNS seeds, speaks a framed message protocol that begins with a version handshake, learns more addresses from its peers and keeps different kinds of connection (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_PeersAreEqual a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Peers are equal"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PeersAreEqual" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_PeersAreEqual> ;
    skos:definition "A script that knows nothing of Bitcoin Core except the wire format introduced itself to a Bitcoin Core 31.1 node as an equal: the node answered with its version message as /Satoshi:31.1.0/, then wtxidrelay, sendaddrv2 and verack, all framed with the magic bytes fabfb5da of the private test chain."@en .

pg:S_PlaintextV1 a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Plaintext v1 transport"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PlaintextV1" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_PlaintextV1> ;
    skos:definition "On the old v1 transport the first bytes sent were the magic fabfb5da, the word version is visible in the stream, and the session identifier of the connection is empty."@en .

pg:S_PrivacyAndTransport a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Privacy and transport"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PrivacyAndTransport" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_PrivacyAndTransport> ;
    skos:definition "The sixth section is about who can see a node's traffic and where it is: the encrypted v2 transport, which is the largest change since the book, the Tor, I2P and CJDNS networks and the attacks on the network (Mehta et al., 2019)."@en .

pg:S_PrivateRelay a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Private relay"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PrivateRelay" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_PrivateRelay> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks how miners got blocks around faster before and beside compact blocks: private relay networks and the forward error correction of FIBRE (Corallo, 2016)."@en .

pg:S_ProtocolVersionNumber a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Protocol version number"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ProtocolVersionNumber" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_ProtocolVersionNumber> ;
    skos:definition "The protocol version of Bitcoin Core 31.1 is 70016, both in the source constant PROTOCOL_VERSION and in the version message, and wtxid relay needs 70016 too, whereas the book still shows the example version 70002 of 2013."@en .

pg:S_PrunedNodeServices a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Pruned node services"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PrunedNodeServices" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_PrunedNodeServices> ;
    skos:definition "A pruned node of the test network drops the NETWORK bit and advertises only WITNESS, NETWORK_LIMITED and P2P_V2, in both its networkinfo and its version message, which tells peers it can serve only the most recent 288 blocks as BIP 159 defines."@en .

pg:S_RaceAndAnnouncement a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Race and announcement"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RaceAndAnnouncement" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_RaceAndAnnouncement> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks why the speed of block announcement matters, because a slow announcement lets a rival block be found, and how the sendheaders message saves one round trip (Daftuar, 2015)."@en .

pg:S_ReachableNodesToday a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Reachable nodes today"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ReachableNodesToday" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_ReachableNodesToday> ;
    skos:definition "The Bitnodes snapshot of 2026-10-15 lists 25,361 reachable nodes, of which 12,488 are onion services, 7,473 IPv4, 4,208 I2P, 1,191 IPv6 and 1 CJDNS, so 8,664 are reachable on the clearnet, while the book still speaks of about 10,000 listening nodes."@en .

pg:S_RelayFlagSilencesTransactions a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Relay flag silences transactions"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RelayFlagSilencesTransactions" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_RelayFlagSilencesTransactions> ;
    skos:definition "A peer that connects with the relay flag false receives no transaction announcements at all: of two transactions the node announced two to the normal peer, none to the relay-false peer and one to the peer whose feefilter was 5 satoshis per virtual byte."@en .

pg:S_RelayNetworkHistory a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Relay network history"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RelayNetworkHistory" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_RelayNetworkHistory> ;
    skos:definition "The book names the relay network of Matt Corallo created in 2015 and replaced in 2016, and the FIBRE site describes the successor as a UDP transport with forward error correction and compact blocks; neither could be run in the sandbox."@en .

pg:S_RoutingAttack a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Routing attack"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RoutingAttack" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_RoutingAttack> ;
    skos:definition "The routing attack of Apostolaki and colleagues needs control of network routes and not of nodes, and the paper states that it can isolate about 50 percent of the mining power."@en .

pg:S_SendheadersAnnouncement a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Sendheaders announcement"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SendheadersAnnouncement" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_SendheadersAnnouncement> ;
    skos:definition "A new block is announced with an inv message to a peer that did not send sendheaders and with a headers message to a peer that did, as BIP 130 defines."@en .

pg:S_ServiceBitsOfNetwork a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Service bits of network"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ServiceBitsOfNetwork" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_ServiceBitsOfNetwork> ;
    skos:definition "The service bits of the census count 22,402 nodes with NETWORK, 24,582 with WITNESS, 25,322 with NETWORK_LIMITED, 7,905 with COMPACT_FILTERS, 4,308 with BLOOM and 21,768 with P2P_V2, which is 85.8 percent of all nodes."@en .

pg:S_ShortIdsAreSixBytes a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Short ids are six bytes"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ShortIdsAreSixBytes" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_ShortIdsAreSixBytes> ;
    skos:definition "A block of 13 transactions was relayed as a compact block of 332 bytes instead of 3,062 (10.84 percent), with 12 short ids of 6 bytes each, equal to the ones the script computed with SipHash-2-4 (the reference vector a129ca6149be45e5)."@en .

pg:S_SixConfirmationsOdds a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Odds after six confirmations"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SixConfirmationsOdds" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_SixConfirmationsOdds> ;
    skos:definition "Nakamoto's calculation gives an attacker with 10 percent of the hash rate a chance of 0.0002428 to catch up from 6 blocks behind, and one with 30 percent a chance of 0.1321; a header chain for 6 blocks per hour of a year is 80 x 6 x 24 x 365 = 4,204,800 bytes."@en .

pg:S_SoftwareShare a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Software share"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SoftwareShare" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_SoftwareShare> ;
    skos:definition "Of the 25,361 reachable nodes, 25,278 (99.67 percent) report a /Satoshi: user agent, 4,083 of them Bitcoin Knots and 21,195 (83.57 percent of all) plain Bitcoin Core, the most common single agent being /Satoshi:31.1.0/ with 7,442 nodes; the book's remark that more than 95 percent of full nodes run Bitcoin Core is still true if Knots is counted as its fork."@en .

pg:S_Spv a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Simplified payment verification (SPV)"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Spv" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_Spv> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks how a client with only the headers can be sure of a payment: the small size of the headers, the Merkle path and the odds of an attacker catching up as Nakamoto calculated them (Nakamoto, 2008)."@en .

pg:S_Syncing a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Syncing"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#Syncing" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_Syncing> ;
    skos:definition "The fourth section shows how a node that was offline, or never online, gets the chain: headers first, then blocks inside a download window, with the checks that stop a peer from filling the node's memory (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_TorHiddenService a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Tor hidden service"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TorHiddenService" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_TorHiddenService> ;
    skos:definition "A node creates a Tor onion service by itself when Tor's control port is there (the option -listenonion defaults to 1), 12,488 nodes of the census are onion addresses, and the book's log line tor: ADD_ONION successful is still the message."@en .

pg:S_ToyBloomFilter a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Toy bloom filter"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ToyBloomFilter" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_ToyBloomFilter> ;
    skos:definition "A toy filter of 16 bits and 3 hash functions with two patterns added has 6 bits set, answers yes for both patterns, and for random patterns it falsely said maybe 5.3 percent of the time (the formula gives 3.1 percent), while it can say a definite no."@en .

pg:S_TxAnnouncementByWtxid a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Transaction announcement by wtxid"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TxAnnouncementByWtxid" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_TxAnnouncementByWtxid> ;
    skos:definition "With wtxid relay the transactions were announced with inv entries of type 5 (MSG_WTX) to the normal peer and to the feefilter peer, and the transaction the script received had the txid of the one with the high fee rate."@en .

pg:S_UnauthenticatedTransport a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Unauthenticated transport"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#UnauthenticatedTransport" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_UnauthenticatedTransport> ;
    skos:definition "BIP 324 encrypts the connection but does not authenticate the peer, so a man in the middle remains possible and only passive eavesdropping is stopped; its predecessor BIP 151 is closed."@en .

pg:S_V2DefaultHistory a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Default history of the v2 transport"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#V2DefaultHistory" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_V2DefaultHistory> ;
    skos:definition "Bitcoin Core 26.0 added the v2 transport off by default and 27.0 turned it on, DEFAULT_V2_TRANSPORT is true in the source, and 85.8 percent of the nodes of the census advertise P2P_V2, whereas the book says that the traffic is in the clear."@en .

pg:S_VersionMessageFields a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Version message fields"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#VersionMessageFields" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10#S_VersionMessageFields> ;
    skos:definition "The version message of the test node is 102 bytes and carries protocol version 70016, services 3081, the user agent /Satoshi:31.1.0/, the start height 2099 of its chain and the relay flag true, in the eleven fields the script parsed."@en .

pg:CO6 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Explain how transactions are announced and filtered, why every mempool is local, and what a node does with a child that arrives before its parent"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Understand ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can explain how transactions are announced and filtered, why every mempool is local, and what a node does with a child that arrives before its parent."@en .

pg:CO1 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Describe the Bitcoin network as a flat peer-to-peer network of equal nodes, name the services nodes differ in, and read what a census of reachable nodes shows"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Understand ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can describe the Bitcoin network as a flat peer-to-peer network of equal nodes, name the services nodes differ in, and read what a census of reachable nodes shows."@en .

pg:CO2 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Follow how a node joins: DNS seeds, the version handshake and its services, address exchange, and the kinds of connection a node keeps"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can follow how a node joins: DNS seeds, the version handshake and its services, address exchange, and the kinds of connection a node keeps."@en .

pg:CO4 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Compare a light client that checks headers and Merkle paths, the Bloom filters of BIP 37 that leaked privacy, and the block filters of BIPs 157 and 158 that replaced them"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Analyze ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can compare a light client that checks headers and Merkle paths, the Bloom filters of BIP 37 that leaked privacy, and the block filters of BIPs 157 and 158 that replaced them."@en .

pg:CO5 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Explain what the encrypted v2 transport and the Tor, I2P and CJDNS networks hide, and judge the eclipse and routing attacks on the network"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Evaluate ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can explain what the encrypted v2 transport and the Tor, I2P and CJDNS networks hide, and judge the eclipse and routing attacks on the network."@en .

pg:CO3 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Explain how a block travels (announcement, compact blocks, private relay) and how a node syncs headers first and blocks after, and judge why speed matters for rival blocks"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Analyze ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch10/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can explain how a block travels (announcement, compact blocks, private relay) and how a node syncs headers first and blocks after, and judge why speed matters for rival blocks."@en .

